  -- ── issue mappings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- pack-generation: derived — the fingerprint moves with these rows; every
  --   affected member's pack retires and rebuilds on the next read. Confirmed
  --   with scripts/test-vr-pack-key-version.mjs.
  --
  -- 12 of the 28 rows are primary and 16 are secondary, which is a rule 30
  -- decision rather than a convenience, and the honest version of it is not
  -- 'every key gains a primary'. 4 of the 6 keys gain one from this wave —
  -- border_security, restraint, tough_on_crime, war_powers. TWO DO NOT:
  -- deportations gains a single secondary row, and strong_defense gains seven
  -- secondary rows and no primary, because a resolution ordering forces out of
  -- one engagement is not a vote on force levels, procurement or posture. What
  -- that costs was read out of the shipped code rather than assumed, because
  -- the gate everybody remembers is no longer a gate: stance-helpers.js:1573
  -- records that the last primary lock on characterisation was removed on
  -- purpose — isPrimary is a label on the bill, printable everywhere and
  -- consultable by nothing — and the surviving use at stance-helpers.js:1598
  -- only decides whether _rdPackageNote's sentence prints beside the finding.
  -- So a secondary-only key cannot suppress or downgrade a reading. Measured
  -- on the members rather than on the structure: of the 98 this wave touches
  -- on strong_defense and the 116 on deportations, ZERO become newly readable
  -- on those keys and ZERO start printing the package sentence, because every
  -- one already holds a primary act on the key from an earlier instrument. The
  -- guards below assert what is structurally true — each key this wave writes
  -- carries a primary somewhere in the corpus, and the exact split of which
  -- keys this wave supplies one for — so that flipping a primary to a
  -- secondary later trips a wall instead of quietly moving a sentence onto a
  -- hundred readings.

  -- S.J.Res. 83 · restraint w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   The resolution's operative section directs the President to remove United
  --   States Armed Forces from hostilities that Congress has not authorized, and
  --   its saving clause preserves only defence against an attack already under
  --   way or imminent. The instrument's whole command is withdrawal, so the
  --   direction on the key is not inferred from a title: a yea ordered forces
  --   out and a nay left them in. This is the face S.J.Res. 59 already carries
  --   for the identical form and the identical question -- restraint w100
  --   primary yea_supports, war_powers w70, strong_defense w55 opposed -- and it
  --   is copied rather than re-derived, because runbook rule 25 says the same
  --   subject may not be mapped two ways without a written reason and there is
  --   no reason here: this is the same subject.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- S.J.Res. 83 · war_powers w70 secondary yea_supports
  --   A privileged joint resolution under section 5(c) of the War Powers
  --   Resolution, which reached the floor only because the Foreign Relations
  --   Committee was discharged, and what it asserts is that the decision to
  --   authorize belongs to Congress. war_powers sits in _RD_NO_POLE in
  --   stance-helpers.js, so this row is inventory and never prints a direction:
  --   it records that the member voted on a war-powers instrument, which is
  --   true, and it makes no claim about where they stand. Filed at 70 rather
  --   than 100 because the operative command is the removal of forces rather
  --   than a change to the authorization machinery itself -- the weight S.J.Res.
  --   59 carries for the same reason.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- S.J.Res. 83 · strong_defense w55 secondary yea_opposes
  --   A yea withdraws United States forces from an ongoing engagement absent a
  --   vote of Congress, and that is what strong_defense reads as its opposed
  --   direction. The weight is held at 55, the weight S.J.Res. 59 carries,
  --   because the resolution takes no position on force levels, procurement,
  --   budgets or posture; it reaches one engagement and stops. Recording it
  --   lower would erase a real vote and recording it higher would turn a
  --   single-engagement question into a defence-posture score.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;
  --
  --   DECLINED on S.J.Res. 83 — foreign_balance: A WRITTEN DEPARTURE from the
  --   precedent face, and the only one. S.J.Res. 59 carries foreign_balance w45
  --   from the July 2026 landmark pass with the rationale 'Concerns the scope
  --   and restraint of U.S. military involvement abroad.' F7 does not copy it.
  --   _rdSuppressedKey() in stance-helpers.js returns 'balance_key' for any key
  --   ending in _balance, which means such a row is permanently unread: it can
  --   never print a direction and never counts toward a characterised reading.
  --   F6's shipped wall was 'no *_balance key was used to park anything', and
  --   seven more measures times one unread row is exactly the parking F6
  --   forbade. Dropping it changes no reader-visible sentence, because the row
  --   it would create is one the reader never sees. The three rows that ship
  --   carry the whole of what the vote says.
  --   DECLINED on S.J.Res. 83 — america_first_fp: Refused because the repository
  --   already removed it from this instrument by hand. Migration
  --   20260904000000_vr_split_umbrella_issue_keys.sql re-keyed S.J.Res. 59's
  --   america_first_fp w70 to war_powers, and its own comment names the five
  --   war-powers instruments it moved. Adding the umbrella key back on a fresh
  --   measure of the same form would undo a settled re-key from outside it,
  --   which runbook rule 21 exists to prevent.
  --   DECLINED on S.J.Res. 83 — israel_support: Not adjacent, not applicable,
  --   and named because the corpus's other Middle East instruments carry it.
  --   None of these resolutions mentions Israel, and the Iran ones reach only
  --   United States forces in hostilities with Iran. Runbook rule 41: a cousin
  --   refusal is settled on the shipped scope note, never on the fact that two
  --   subjects share a region.

  -- S.J.Res. 90 · restraint w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   The resolution's operative section directs the President to remove United
  --   States Armed Forces from hostilities within or against Venezuela that
  --   Congress has not authorized, and its saving clause preserves only defence
